\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Four-phase analysis of a finite signal. The channels are decimated coordinate packets. Interleaving them gives the original signal exactly.}}{4}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The exact transfer matrix for a seven-tap FIR filter in four phases. Every cell records the delays and coefficients carrying one input packet into one output packet.}}{6}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Exact address comparison for a two-phase split. A delay of one original sample sends the odd channel into the even channel with a coarse delay, and the even channel into the odd channel without a coarse delay. A one-step channel delay moves both original residues by two samples. All plotted values are integer sample addresses.}}{7}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces A piecewise-smooth signal and its first Haar low/detail packets. The low channel records local averages. The detail channel records adjacent-sample differences, including pairs that cross a jump.}}{9}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Energy of the sixteen depth-four Haar packet addresses for the test signal. The packet address is an operator word in the common seed grammar.}}{11}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces An exact rational eight-coordinate example of retained packet cancellation. The supplied jump packet is kept, while the linear operator averages adjacent pairs in the smooth component. The two error vectors agree coordinate by coordinate. This model illustrates cancellation and does not test packet detection or a Fourier convergence rate.}}{11}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces One-dimensional reconstruction and absolute error. The known packet removes the edge from the global spectral approximation. The estimated packet is visibly better than the global reconstruction in this limited test but retains smooth-background estimation error.}}{14}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Error as spectral resolution increases. The known packet follows only the smooth approximation error. The estimated reconstruction reaches a floor determined by background and amplitude estimation.}}{15}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces A sharp disk on a smooth background. Global Fourier truncation produces a bright boundary halo and oscillatory interior and exterior error. Retaining the exact edge packet leaves only the smooth-background approximation error.}}{16}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The limited data-driven experiment. The observed image alone is thresholded into two intensity classes, cleaned topologically, harmonically filled, spectrally smoothed, and recombined with an estimated jump.}}{17}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Known-packet stress test with curved boundaries, corners, overlap, and different jump amplitudes. The exact packet is not a binary mask multiplied by one amplitude. It is the complete piecewise packet.}}{18}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Sparse approximation of the test signal. With very few coefficients the global Fourier basis is initially better. After the edge packets are resolved, the Haar packet error decays much faster in this example.}}{19}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces A globally optimal mixed-depth Haar packet pruning. The selected antichain covers the full tree, so its synthesis is exact even though different branches stop at different depths.}}{23}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The dynamic-programming optimum agrees with exhaustive enumeration. A positive gap larger than twice the declared perturbation envelope certifies that the selected tree is stable.}}{24}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Norms of pairwise product interactions for five residue phases. The label in each cell gives the output phase $(i+j)\bmod 5$.}}{25}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The classical Smith grid equipped with sixteen exact phase rays and four radial seed orbits. The Cayley transform creates the chart. The projector calculus supplies the nested cyclic coordinate system.}}{28}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces The same reflection circle at phase orders $2,4,8,16,32$. Each coarse phase ray appears in every finer grid whose order is a multiple of its order. The exact residue-projector refinement law concerns coefficient packets, not sums of impedance points.}}{29}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces The proposed symmetric refinement weight becomes large near the open- and short-circuit boundary points. It is a condition-based adaptive coordinate, not an asserted optimal sampling law.}}{31}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Error restricted to a narrow boundary band. Global truncation concentrates large error on the jump. The known-packet reconstruction has only the small smooth-packet error in the same band.}}{31}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Proof and claim boundary. Finite adaptive-tree selection is proved. The remaining open rows concern universal recovery and infinite-tree or asymptotic selection.}}{32}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E1}{\ignorespaces A signal and its four exact residue channels. Interleaving the channels returns the original samples without approximation.}}{46}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E2}{\ignorespaces Absolute tap weight in the transfer blocks of the four phase operator in \cref {ex:four_phase_fir}.}}{49}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E3}{\ignorespaces Pointwise singular value bounds govern both the original frame and its perturbed analysis bank.}}{52}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E4}{\ignorespaces A complete packet tree and one admissible pruning. Retained nodes may occur at different depths.}}{55}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E5}{\ignorespaces An additive best basis recursion compares the cost of retaining a packet with the total cost of splitting it.}}{57}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E6}{\ignorespaces Truncated power packets encode jumps in a function and in its successive derivatives.}}{59}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E7}{\ignorespaces A global Fourier approximation must represent the jumps. A retained packet approximation applies the Fourier truncation only to the smooth component.}}{61}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E8}{\ignorespaces An overlap matrix records both correct regions and every type of misclassification. The exact packet norm weights each entry by the corresponding value difference.}}{63}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E9}{\ignorespaces A sufficient threshold interval from exact rational contrast bounds. With the displayed model parameters, smooth and noise contrasts are at most $1/10$ and jump contrasts are at least $9/10$. Every threshold strictly between those values separates the cells under the sampling assumptions stated below. The figure does not assert an optimal threshold for other observation models.}}{65}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E10}{\ignorespaces Separated jumps create isolated adjacent sample contrasts above the smooth and noise background.}}{68}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E11}{\ignorespaces The smooth contribution decays like \(L^{3/2}\), while a separated jump contribution decays like \(L^{1/2}\).}}{71}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E12}{\ignorespaces The boundaries of the two grids interlace. Every jump is well inside a cell in at least one grid.}}{72}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E13}{\ignorespaces Global Fourier truncation represents the jump and exhibits an oscillatory boundary layer. Packet separation retains the jump and approximates only the smooth component.}}{76}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E14}{\ignorespaces One finite sparse approximation comparison. Fourier coordinates are better at the smallest budget, while the fixed Haar packet basis becomes better after localized edge coordinates are resolved.}}{78}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E15}{\ignorespaces The cyclic action is an ordinary phase rotation in the reflection disk and a fractional linear action in the impedance half plane.}}{80}{figure.caption.46}%
